024	JER	052	024	And the maister of the chyualrie took Saraie, the firste preest, and Sophonye, the secounde preest, and three keperis of the vestiarie.
024	JER	052	025	And of the citee he took o chast seruaunt and onest, that was souereyn on the men werriours; and seuene men of hem that sien the face of the kyng, whiche weren foundun in the citees; and a scryuen, prince of knyytis, that preuyde yonge knyytis; and sixti men of the puple of the lond, that weren foundun in the myddis of the citee.
024	JER	052	026	Forsothe Nabusardan, the maistir of chyualrie, took hem, and brouyte hem to the kyng of Babiloyne in Reblatha.
024	JER	052	027	And the kyng of Babiloyne smoot hem, and killide hem in Reblatha, in the lond of Emath; and Juda was translatid fro his lond.
024	JER	052	028	This is the puple, whom Nabugodonosor translatide in the seuenthe yeer; Jewis, thre thousynde and thre and twenti.
024	JER	052	029	In the eiytenthe yeer, Nabugodonosor translatide fro Jerusalem eiyte hundrid and two and thritti persoones.
024	JER	052	030	In the thre and twentithe yeer of Nabugodonosor, Nabusardan, the maister of chyualrie, translatide seuene hundrid and fyue and fourti persoones of Jewis. Therfor alle the persoones weren foure thousynde and sixe hundrid.
024	JER	052	031	And it was doon, in the seuene and threttithe yeer of the passyng ouer of Joachym, kyng of Juda, in the tweluethe monethe, in the fyue and twentithe dai of the monethe, Euylmerodach, kyng of Babiloyne, reiside in that yeer of his rewme the heed of Joachym, kyng of Juda; and ledde hym out of the hous of the prisoun,
024	JER	052	032	and spak good thingis with hym. And he settide the trone of him aboue the trones of kyngis, that weren after hym in Babiloyne,
024	JER	052	033	and chaungide the clothis of his prisoun. And Joachym eet breed bifore hym euere, in alle the daies of his lijf;
024	JER	052	034	and hise metis, euerlastynge metis weren youun to hym of the kyng of Babiloyne, ordeyned bi ech dai, til to the dai of his deth, in alle the daies of his lijf. And it was don, aftir that Israel was led in to caitiftee, and Jerusalem was distried, Jeremye, the profete, sat wepinge, and biweilide Jerusalem with this lamentacioun; and he siyyide, and weilide with bitter soule, and seide.
#
# BOOK	025	LAM	Lamentations	Lamentations
025	LAM	001	001	Aleph. Hou sittith aloone the citee ful of puple? the ladi of folkis is maad as a widewe; the prince of prouynces is maad vndir tribute.
025	LAM	001	002	Beth. It wepynge wepte in the niyt, and the teeris therof ben in `the chekis therof; `noon is of alle the dereworthe therof, that coumfortith it; alle the frendis therof forsoken it, and ben maad enemyes to it.
025	LAM	001	003	Gymel. Juda passide fro turment and multitude of seruage, it dwellide among hethene men, and foond no reste; alle the pursueris therof token it among angwischis.
025	LAM	001	004	Deleth. The weies of Sion mourenen, for no men comen to the solempnytee; alle the yatis therof ben distried, the prestis therof weilen; the vergyns therof ben defoulid, and it is oppressid with bitternesse.
025	LAM	001	005	He. The enemyes therof ben maad in the heed, and the enemyes therof ben maad riche, for the Lord spak on it. For the multitude of wickidnessis therof the litle children therof ben led in to caitiftee, bifore the face of the troblere.
025	LAM	001	006	Vau. And al the fairnesse of the douyter of Syon yede out fro the douyter of Sion; the princes therof ben maad as rammes not fyndynge lesewis; and yeden forth withouten strengthe bifore the face of the suere.
025	LAM	001	007	Zai. And Jerusalem bithouyte on the daies of hir affliccioun and of trespassyng, and on alle hir desirable thingis whiche it hadde fro elde daies; whanne the puple therof felle doun in the hond of enemyes, and noon helpere was; enemyes sien it, and scorneden the sabatis therof.
025	LAM	001	008	Heth. Jerusalem synnede a synne, therfor it was maad vnstidfast; alle that glorifieden it forsoken it, for thei sien the schenschipe therof; forsothe it weilide, and was turned a bak.
025	LAM	001	009	Theth. The filthis therof ben in the feet therof, and it hadde no mynde of hir ende; it was putte doun greetli, and hadde no coumfortour; Lord, se thou my turment, for the enemye is reisid.
025	LAM	001	010	Joth. The enemye putte his hond to alle desirable thingis therof; for it siy hethene men entride in to thi seyntuarie, of which thou haddist comaundid, that thei schulden not entre in to thi chirche.
025	LAM	001	011	Caph. Al the puple therof was weilinge and sekynge breed, thei yauen alle preciouse thingis for mete, to coumforte the soule; se thou, Lord, and biholde, for Y am maad vijl.
025	LAM	001	012	Lameth. A! alle ye that passen bi the weie, perseyue, and se, if ony sorewe is as my sorewe; for he gaderide awei grapis fro me, as the Lord spak in the day of wraththe of his strong veniaunce.
025	LAM	001	013	Men. Fro an hiy he sente fier in my boonys, and tauyte me; he spredde a brood a net to my feet, he turnede me a bak; he settide me desolat, meddlid togidere al dai with mourenyng.
025	LAM	001	014	Nun. The yok of my wickidnessis wakide in the hond of hym, tho ben foldid togidere, and put on my necke; my vertu is maad feble; the Lord yaf me in the hond, fro which Y schal not mowe rise.
025	LAM	001	015	Sameth. The Lord took awei alle my worschipful men fro the myddis of me; he clepide tyme ayens me, that he schulde al to-foule my chosun men; the Lord stampide a pressour to the virgyn, the douytir of Juda.
025	LAM	001	016	Ayn. Therfor Y am wepynge, and myn iye is ledynge doun watir; for a coumfortour, conuertynge my soule, is maad fer fro me; my sones ben maad lost, for the enemye hadde the maistrie.
025	LAM	001	017	Phe. Sion spredde a brood hise hondis, noon is that coumfortith it; the Lord sente ayenus Jacob enemyes therof, in the cumpas therof; Jerusalem is maad as defoulid with vncleene blood among hem.
025	LAM	001	018	Sade. The Lord is iust, for Y terride his mouth to wrathfulnesse; alle puplis, Y biseche, here ye, and se my sorewe; my virgyns and my yonge men yeden forth in to caitiftee.
025	LAM	001	019	Coth. I clepide my frendis, and thei disseyueden me; my prestis and myn elde men in the citee ben wastid; for thei souyten mete to hem silf, to coumforte hir lijf.
025	LAM	001	020	Res. Se thou, Lord, for Y am troblid, my wombe is disturblid; myn herte is distried in my silf, for Y am ful of bittirnesse; swerd sleeth with outforth, and lijk deth is at hoome.
025	LAM	001	021	Syn. Thei herden, that Y make ynward weilyng, and noon is that coumfortith me; alle myn enemyes herden myn yuel, thei ben glad, for thou hast do; thou hast brouyt a dai of coumfort, and thei schulen be maad lijk me.
025	LAM	001	022	Tau. Al the yuel of hem entre byfore thee, and gadere thou grapis awei fro hem, as thou hast gaderid grapis awei fro me; for my wickidnessis, for my weilyngis ben manye, and myn herte is mornynge.
025	LAM	002	001	Aleph. Hou hath the Lord hilid the douyter of Sion with derknesse in his strong veniaunce? he hath caste doun fro heuene in to erthe the noble citee of Israel; and bithouyte not on the stool of hise feet, in the dai of his strong veniaunce.
025	LAM	002	002	Beth. The Lord castide doun, and sparide not alle the faire thingis of Jacob; he distried in his strong veniaunce the strengthis of the virgyn of Juda, and castide doun in to erthe; he defoulide the rewme, and the princes therof.
025	LAM	002	003	Gymel. He brak in the ire of his strong veniaunce al the horn of Israel; he turnede a bak his riyt hond fro the face of the enemy; and he kyndlide in Jacob, as fier of flawme deuowrynge in cumpas.
025	LAM	002	004	Deleth. He as an enemye bente his bouwe, he as an aduersarie made stidfast his riyt hond; and he killide al thing that was fair in siyt in the tabernacle of the douytir of Sion; he schedde out his indignacioun as fier.
025	LAM	002	005	He. The Lord is maad as an enemy; he castide doun Israel, he castide doun alle the wallis therof; he destriede the strengthis therof, and fillide in the douyter of Juda a man maad low, and a womman maad low.
025	LAM	002	006	Vau. And he scateride his tent as a gardyn, he distried his tabernacle; the Lord yaf to foryetyng in Sion a feeste dai, and sabat; and the kyng and prest in to schenschipe, and in to the indignacioun of his strong veniaunce.
025	LAM	002	007	Zai. The Lord puttide awei his auter, he curside his halewyng; he bitook in to the hondis of enemy the wallis of the touris therof; thei yauen vois in the hous of the Lord, as in a solempne dai.
025	LAM	002	008	Heth. The Lord thouyte to distrie the wal of the douyter of Sion; he stretchide forth his coorde, and turnede not awei his hond fro perdicioun; the forwal, ether the outerward, mourenyde, and the wal was distried togidere.
025	LAM	002	009	Teth. The yatis therof ben piyt in the erthe, he loste and al to-brak the barris therof; the kyng therof and the princes therof among hethene men; the lawe is not, and the profetis therof founden not of the Lord a visioun.
025	LAM	002	010	Joth. Thei saten in erthe, the elde men of the douytir of Sion weren stille; thei bispreynten her heedis with aische, the eldere men of Juda ben girt with hairis; the virgyns of Juda castiden doun to erthe her heedis.
025	LAM	002	011	Caph. Myn iyen failiden for teeris, myn entrails weren disturblid; my mawe was sched out in erthe on the sorewe of the douyter of my puple; whanne a litil child and soukynge failide in the stretis of the citee.
025	LAM	002	012	Lameth. Thei seiden to her modris, Where is wheete, and wyn? whanne thei failiden as woundid men in the stretis of the citee; whanne thei senten out her soulis in the bosum of her modris.
025	LAM	002	013	Men. To whom schal Y comparisoun thee? ether to whom schal Y licne thee, thou douyter of Jerusalem? to whom schal Y make thee euene, and schal Y coumforte thee, thou virgyn, the douyter of Sion? for whi thi sorewe is greet as the see; who schal do medicyn to thee?
025	LAM	002	014	Nun. Thi profetis sien to thee false thingis, and fonned; and openyden not thi wickidnesse, that thei schulden stire thee to penaunce; but thei sien to thee false takyngis, and castyngis out.
025	LAM	002	015	Sameth. Alle men passynge on the weie flappiden with hondis on thee; thei hissiden, and mouyden her heed on the douyter of Jerusalem; and seiden, This is the citee of perfit fairnesse, the ioie of al erthe.
025	LAM	002	016	Ayn. Alle thin enemyes openyden her mouth on thee; thei hissiden, and gnaistiden with her teeth, and seiden, We schulen deuoure; lo! this is the dai which we abididen, we founden, we sien.
025	LAM	002	017	Phe. The Lord dide tho thingis whiche he thouyte, he fillide hise word which he hadde comaundid fro elde daies; he distriede, and sparide not; and made glad the enemy on thee, and enhaunside the horn of thin enemyes.
025	LAM	002	018	Sade. The herte of hem criede to the Lord, on the wallis of the douyter of Syon; leede thou forth teeris as a stronde, bi dai and niyt; yyue thou not reste to thee, nether the appil of thin iye be stille.
025	LAM	002	019	Coph. Rise thou togidere, herie thou in the nyyt, in the begynnyng of wakyngis; schede out thin herte as watir, bifore the siyt of the Lord; reise thin hondis to hym for the soulis of thi litle children, that failiden for hungur in the heed of alle meetyngis of weies.
025	LAM	002	020	Res. Se thou, Lord, and byholde, whom thou hast maad so bare; therfor whether wymmen schulen ete her fruyt, litle children at the mesure of an hond? for a prest and profete is slayn in the seyntuarie of the Lord.
025	LAM	002	021	Syn. A child and an elde man laien on the erthe withoutforth; my virgyns and my yonge men fellen doun bi swerd; thou hast slayn hem in the dai of thi strong veniaunce, thou smotist `and didist no merci.
025	LAM	002	022	Thau. Thou clepidist, as to a solempne dai, hem that maden me aferd of cumpas; and noon was that ascapide in the dai of the strong veniaunce of the Lord, and was left; myn enemy wastide hem, whiche Y fedde, and nurschide up.
025	LAM	003	001	Aleph. I am a man seynge my pouert in the yerde of his indignacioun.
025	LAM	003	002	Aleph. He droof me, and brouyte in to derknessis, and not in to liyt.
025	LAM	003	003	Aleph. Oneli he turnede in to me, and turnede togidere his hond al dai.
025	LAM	003	004	Beth. He made eld my skyn, and my fleisch; he al to-brak my boonys.
025	LAM	003	005	Beth. He bildid in my cumpas, and he cumpasside me with galle and trauel.
025	LAM	003	006	Beth. He settide me in derk places, as euerlastynge deed men.
025	LAM	003	007	Gymel. He bildide aboute ayens me, that Y go not out; he aggregide my gyues.
025	LAM	003	008	Gymel. But and whanne Y crie and preye, he hath excludid my preier.
025	LAM	003	009	Gymel. He closide togidere my weies with square stoonus; he distriede my pathis.
025	LAM	003	010	Deleth. He is maad a bere settinge aspies to me, a lioun in hid places.
025	LAM	003	011	Deleth. He distriede my pathis, and brak me; he settide me desolat.
025	LAM	003	012	Deleth. He bente his bowe, and settide me as a signe to an arowe.
025	LAM	003	013	He. He sente in my reynes the douytris of his arowe caas.
025	LAM	003	014	He. Y am maad in to scorn to al the puple, the song of hem al dai.
025	LAM	003	015	He. He fillide me with bitternesses; he gretli fillide me with wermod.
025	LAM	003	016	Vau. He brak at noumbre my teeth; he fedde me with aische.
025	LAM	003	017	Vau. And my soule is putte awei; Y haue foryete goodis.
025	LAM	003	018	Vau. And Y seide, Myn ende perischide, and myn hope fro the Lord.
025	LAM	003	019	Zai. Haue thou mynde on my pouert and goyng ouer, and on wermod and galle.
025	LAM	003	020	Zai. Bi mynde Y schal be myndeful; and my soule schal faile in me.
025	LAM	003	021	Zai. Y bithenkynge these thingis in myn herte, schal hope in God.
025	LAM	003	022	Heth. The mercies of the Lord ben manye, for we ben not wastid; for whi hise merciful doyngis failiden not.
025	LAM	003	023	Heth. Y knew in the morewtid; thi feith is miche.
025	LAM	003	024	Heth. My soule seide, The Lord is my part; therfor Y schal abide hym.
025	LAM	003	025	Teth. The Lord is good to hem that hopen in to hym, to a soule sekynge hym.
025	LAM	003	026	Teth. It is good to abide with stilnesse the helthe of God.
025	LAM	003	027	Teth. It is good to a man, whanne he hath bore the yok fro his yongthe.
025	LAM	003	028	Joth. He schal sitte aloone, and he schal be stille; for he reiside hym silf aboue hym silf.
025	LAM	003	029	Joth. He schal sette his mouth in dust, if perauenture hope is.
025	LAM	003	030	Joth. He schal yyue the cheke to a man that smytith hym; he schal be fillid with schenschipis.
025	LAM	003	031	Caph. For the Lord schal not putte awei with outen ende.
025	LAM	003	032	Caph. For if he castide awei, and he schal do merci bi the multitude of hise mercies.
025	LAM	003	033	Caph. For he makide not low of his herte; and castide not awei the sones of men. Lameth.
025	LAM	003	034	That he schulde al to-foule vndur hise feet alle the boundun men of erthe. Lameth.
025	LAM	003	035	That he schulde bowe doun the dom of man, in the siyt of the cheer of the hiyeste.
025	LAM	003	036	Lameth. That he schulde peruerte a man in his dom, the Lord knew not.
025	LAM	003	037	Men. Who is this that seide, that a thing schulde be don, whanne the Lord comaundide not?
025	LAM	003	038	Men. Nether goodis nether yuels schulen go out of the mouth of the hiyeste.
025	LAM	003	039	Men. What grutchide a man lyuynge, a man for hise synnes?
025	LAM	003	040	Nun. Serche we oure weies, and seke we, and turne we ayen to the Lord.
025	LAM	003	041	Nun. Reise we oure hertis with hondis, to the Lord in to heuenes.
025	LAM	003	042	Nun. We han do wickidli, and han terrid thee to wraththe; therfor thou art not able to be preied.
025	LAM	003	043	Sameth. Thou hilidist in stronge veniaunce, and smitidist vs; thou killidist, and sparidist not.
025	LAM	003	044	Sameth. Thou settidist a clowde to thee, that preier passe not.
025	LAM	003	045	Sameth. Thou settidist me, drawing vp bi the roote, and castynge out, in the myddis of puplis.
025	LAM	003	046	Ayn. Alle enemyes openyden her mouth on vs.
025	LAM	003	047	Ayn. Inward drede and snare is maad to vs, profesie and defoulyng.
025	LAM	003	048	Ayn. Myn iyen ledden doun departyngis of watris, for the defoulyng of the douyter of my puple.
025	LAM	003	049	Phe. Myn iye was turmentid, and was not stille; for no reste was.
025	LAM	003	050	Phe. Vntil the Lord bihelde, and siy fro heuenes.
025	LAM	003	051	Phe. Myn iye robbide my soule in alle the douytris of my citee.
025	LAM	003	052	Sade. Myn enemyes token me with out cause, bi huntyng as a brid.
025	LAM	003	053	Sade. My lijf slood in to a lake; and thei puttiden a stoon on me.
025	LAM	003	054	Sade. Watris flowiden ouer myn heed; Y seide, Y perischide.
025	LAM	003	055	Coph. Lord, Y clepide to help thi name, fro the laste lake.
025	LAM	003	056	Coph. Thou herdist my vois; turne thou not awei thin eere fro my sobbyng and cries.
025	LAM	003	057	Coph. Thou neiyidist to me in the dai, wherynne Y clepide thee to help; thou seidist, Drede thou not.
025	LAM	003	058	Res. Lord, ayenbiere of my lijf, thou demydist the cause of my soule.
025	LAM	003	059	Res. Lord, thou siest the wickidnesse of hem ayens me; deme thou my doom.
025	LAM	003	060	Res. Thou siest al the woodnesse, alle the thouytis of hem ayenus me.
025	LAM	003	061	Syn. Lord, thou herdist the schenshipis of hem; alle the thouytis of hem ayens me.
025	LAM	003	062	Syn. The lippis of men risynge ayens me, and the thouytis of hem ayens me al dai.
025	LAM	003	063	Syn. Se thou the sittynge and risyng ayen of hem; Y am the salm of hem.
025	LAM	003	064	Thau. Lord, thou schalt yelde while to hem, bi the werkis of her hondis.
025	LAM	003	065	Tau. Thou schalt yyue to hem the scheeld of herte, thi trauel.
025	LAM	003	066	Tau. Lord, thou schalt pursue hem in thi strong veniaunce, and thou schalt defoule hem vndur heuenes.
025	LAM	004	001	Aleph. How is gold maad derk, the beste colour is chaungid? the stonys of the seyntuarie ben scaterid in the heed of alle stretis.
025	LAM	004	002	Beth. The noble sones of Sion, and clothid with the best gold, hou ben thei arettid in to erthene vessels, in to the werk of the hondis of a pottere?
025	LAM	004	003	Gimel. But also lamyes maden nakid her tetis, yauen mylk to her whelpis; the douyter of my puple is cruel, as an ostrig in desert.
025	LAM	004	004	Deleth. The tonge of the soukynge childe cleued to his palat in thirst; litle children axiden breed, and noon was that brak to hem.
025	LAM	004	005	He. Thei that eeten lustfuli, perischiden in weies; thei that weren nurschid in cradels, biclippiden toordis.
025	LAM	004	006	Vau. And the wickidnesse of the douyter of my puple is maad more than the synne of men of Sodom, that was distried in a moment, and hondis token not therynne.
025	LAM	004	007	Zai. Nazareis therof weren whitere than snow, schynyngere than mylk; rodier than elde yuer, fairere than safire.
025	LAM	004	008	Heth. The face of hem was maad blackere than coolis, and thei weren not knowun in stretis; the skyn cleuyde to her boonys, it driede, and was maad as a tre.
025	LAM	004	009	Teth. It was betere to men slayn with swerd, than to men slayn with hungur; for these men wexiden rotun, thei weren wastid of the bareynesse of erthe.
025	LAM	004	010	Joth. The hondis of merciful wymmen sethiden her children; thei weren maad the metis of tho wymmen in the sorewe of the douyter of my puple.
025	LAM	004	011	Caph. The Lord fillide his strong veniaunce, he schedde out the ire of his indignacioun; and the Lord kyndlide a fier in Sion, and it deuouride the foundementis therof.
025	LAM	004	012	Lamet. The kyngis of erthe, and alle dwelleris of the world bileueden not, that an aduersarie and enemy schulde entre bi the yatis of Jerusalem.
025	LAM	004	013	Men. For the synnes of the profetis therof, and for wickidnessis of preestis therof, that schedden out the blood of iust men in the myddis therof.
025	LAM	004	014	Nun. Blynde men erryden in stretis, thei weren defoulid in blood; and whanne thei miyten not go, thei helden her hemmes.
025	LAM	004	015	Samet. Thei crieden to hem, Departe awei, ye defoulide men, departe ye, go ye awei, nyle ye touche; forsothe thei chidden, and weren stirid; thei seiden among hethene men, God schal no more leie to, that he dwelle among hem.
025	LAM	004	016	Ayn. The face of the Lord departide hem, he schal no more leie to, that he biholde hem; thei weren not aschamed of the faces of preestis, nether thei hadden merci on eld men.
025	LAM	004	017	Phe. The while we stoden yit, oure iyen failiden to oure veyn help; whanne we bihelden ententif to a folc, that myyte not saue vs.
025	LAM	004	018	Sade. Oure steppis weren slidir in the weie of oure stretis; oure ende neiyede, oure daies weren fillid, for oure ende cam.
025	LAM	004	019	Coph. Oure pursueris weren swiftere than the eglis of heuene; thei pursueden vs on hillis, thei settiden buschementis to vs in desert.
025	LAM	004	020	Res. The spirit of oure mouth, Crist the Lord, was takun in oure synnes; to whom we seiden, We schulen lyue in thi schadewe among hethene men.
025	LAM	004	021	Syn. Thou douyter of Edom, make ioye, and be glad, that dwellist in the lond of Hus; the cuppe schal come also to thee, thou schalt be maad drunkun, and schalt be maad bare.
025	LAM	004	022	Thau. Thou douyter of Sion, thi wickidnesse is fillid; he schal not adde more, that he make thee to passe ouer; thou douyter of Edom, he schal visite thi wickidnesse, he schal vnhile thi synnes.
025	LAM	005	001	Lord, haue thou mynde what bifelle to vs; se thou, and biholde oure schenschipe.
025	LAM	005	002	Oure eritage is turned to aliens, oure housis ben turned to straungers.
025	LAM	005	003	We ben maad fadirles children with out fadir; oure modris ben as widewis.
025	LAM	005	004	We drunken oure watir for monei, we bouyten oure trees for siluer.
025	LAM	005	005	We weren dryuun bi oure heedis, and reste was not youun to feynt men.
025	LAM	005	006	We yauen hond to Egipt, and to Assiriens, that we schulden be fillid with breed.
025	LAM	005	007	Oure fadris synneden, and ben not, and we baren the wickidnessis of hem.
025	LAM	005	008	Seruauntis weren lordis of vs, and noon was, that ayenbouyte fro the hond of hem.
025	LAM	005	009	In oure lyues we brouyten breed to vs, fro the face of swerd in desert.
025	LAM	005	010	Oure skynne is brent as a furneis, of the face of tempestis of hungur.
025	LAM	005	011	Thei maden low wymmen in Sion, and virgyns in the citees of Juda.
025	LAM	005	012	Princes weren hangid bi the hond; thei weren not aschamed of the faces of elde men.
025	LAM	005	013	Thei mysusiden yonge wexynge men vnchastli, and children fellen doun in tree.
025	LAM	005	014	Elde men failiden fro yatis; yonge men failiden of the queer of singeris.
025	LAM	005	015	The ioie of oure herte failide; oure song is turned in to mourenyng.
025	LAM	005	016	The coroun of oure heed fellen doun; wo to vs! for we synneden.
025	LAM	005	017	Therfor oure herte is maad soreuful, therfor oure iyen ben maad derk.
025	LAM	005	018	For the hil of Sion, for it perischide; foxis yeden in it.
025	LAM	005	019	But thou, Lord, schal dwelle with outen ende; thi seete schal dwelle in generacioun and in to generacioun.
025	LAM	005	020	Whi schalt thou foryete vs with outen ende, schalt thou forsake vs in to lengthe of daies?
025	LAM	005	021	Lord, conuerte thou vs to thee, and we schal be conuertid; make thou newe oure daies, as at the bigynnyng.
025	LAM	005	022	But thou castynge awei hast cast awei vs; thou art wrooth ayens vs greetli.
