# BOOK	059	JAM	James	James
059	JAM	001	001	James, the seruaunt of God, and of oure Lord Jhesu Crist, to the twelue kinredis, that ben in scatering abrood, helthe.
059	JAM	001	002	My britheren, deme ye al ioye, whanne ye fallen in to diuerse temptaciouns, witynge,
059	JAM	001	003	that the preuyng of youre feith worchith pacience;
059	JAM	001	004	and pacience hath a perfit werk, that ye be perfit and hole, and faile in no thing.
059	JAM	001	005	And if ony of you nedith wisdom, axe he of God, which yyueth to alle men largeli, and vpbreidith not; and it schal be youun to hym.
059	JAM	001	006	But axe he in feith, and doute no thing; for he that doutith, is lijk to a wawe of the see, which is moued and borun a boute of wynde.
059	JAM	001	007	Therfor gesse not the ilke man, that he schal take ony thing of the Lord.
059	JAM	001	008	A man dowble in soule is vnstable in alle hise weies.
059	JAM	001	009	And a meke brother haue glorie in his enhaunsyng,
059	JAM	001	010	and a riche man in his lownesse; for as the flour of gras he schal passe.
059	JAM	001	011	The sunne roos vp with heete, and driede the gras, and the flour of it felde doun, and the fairnesse of his chere perischide; and so a riche man welewith in hise weies.
059	JAM	001	012	Blessid is the man, that suffrith temptacioun; for whanne he schal be preued, he schal resseyue the coroun of lijf, which God biheyte to men that louen hym.
059	JAM	001	013	No man whanne he is temptid, seie, that he is temptid of God; for whi God is not a temptere of yuele thingis, for he temptith no man.
059	JAM	001	014	But ech man is temptid, drawun and stirid of his owne coueiting.
059	JAM	001	015	Aftirward coueityng, whanne it hath conseyued, bringith forth synne; but synne, whanne it is fillid, gendrith deth.
059	JAM	001	016	Therfor, my most dereworthe britheren, nyle ye erre.
059	JAM	001	017	Ech good yifte, and ech perfit yifte is from aboue, and cometh doun fro the fadir of liytis, anentis whom is noon other chaungyng, ne ouerschadewyng of reward.
059	JAM	001	018	For wilfulli he bigat vs bi the word of treuthe, that we be a bigynnyng of his creature.
059	JAM	001	019	Wite ye, my britheren moost loued, be ech man swift to here, but slow to speke, and slow to wraththe;
059	JAM	001	020	for the wraththe of man worchith not the riytwisnesse of God.
059	JAM	001	021	For which thing caste ye awei al vnclennesse, and plentee of malice, and in myldenesse resseyue ye the word that is plauntid, that may saue youre soulis.
059	JAM	001	022	But be ye doeris of the word, and not hereris oneli, disseiuynge you silf.
059	JAM	001	023	For if ony man is an herere of the word, and not a doere, this schal be licned to a man that biholdith the cheer of his birthe in a mirour;
059	JAM	001	024	for he bihelde hym silf, and wente awei, and anoon he foryat which he was.
059	JAM	001	025	But he that biholdith in the lawe of perfit fredom, and dwellith in it, and is not maad a foryetful herere, but a doere of werk, this schal be blessid in his dede.
059	JAM	001	026	And if ony man gessith hym silf to be religiouse, and refreyneth not his tunge, but disseyueth his herte, the religioun of him is veyn.
059	JAM	001	027	A clene religioun, and an vnwemmed anentis God and the fadir, is this, to visite fadirles and modirles children, and widewis in her tribulacioun, and to kepe hym silf vndefoulid fro this world.
059	JAM	002	001	Mi britheren, nyle ye haue the feith of oure Lord Jhesu Crist of glorie, in accepcioun of persoones.
059	JAM	002	002	For if a man `that hath a goldun ring, and in a feire clothing, cometh in youre cumpany, and a pore man entrith in a foul clothing,
059	JAM	002	003	and if ye biholden in to hym that is clothid with clere clothing, and if ye seie to hym, Sitte thou here wel; but to the pore man ye seien, Stonde thou there, ethir sitte vndur the stool of my feet; whether ye demen not anentis you silf,
059	JAM	002	004	and ben maad domesmen of wickid thouytis?
059	JAM	002	005	Heere ye, my moost dereworthe britheren, whethir God chees not pore men in this world, riche in feith, and eiris of the kyngdom, that God bihiyte to men that louen him?
059	JAM	002	006	But ye han dispisid the pore man. Whether riche men oppressen not you bi power, and thei drawen you to domes?
059	JAM	002	007	Whether thei blasfemen not the good name, that is clepid to help on you?
059	JAM	002	008	Netheles if ye performen the kingis lawe, bi scripturis, Thou schalt loue thi neiybour as thi silf, ye don wel.
059	JAM	002	009	But if ye taken persones, ye worchen synne, and ben repreued of the lawe, as trespasseris.
059	JAM	002	010	And who euere kepith al the lawe, but offendith in oon, he is maad gilti of alle.
059	JAM	002	011	For he that seide, Thou schalt do no letcherie, seide also, Thou schalt not sle; that if thou doist not letcherie, but thou sleest, thou art maad trespassour of the lawe.
059	JAM	002	012	Thus speke ye, and thus do ye, as bigynnynge to be demyd bi the lawe of fredom.
059	JAM	002	013	For whi dom with out merci is to hym, that doith no mercy; but merci aboue reisith dom.
059	JAM	002	014	Mi britheren, what schal it profite, if ony man seie that he hath feith, but he hath not the werkis? whether feith schal mowe saue hym?
059	JAM	002	015	And if a brother ethir sister be nakid, and han nede of ech daies lyuelode,
059	JAM	002	016	and if ony of you seie to hem, Go ye in pees, be ye maad hoot, and be ye fillid; but if ye yyuen not to hem tho thingis that ben necessarie to bodi, what schal it profite?
059	JAM	002	017	So also feith, if it hath not werkis, is deed in it silf.
059	JAM	002	018	But summan schal seie, Thou hast feith, and Y haue werkis; schewe thou to me thi feith with out werkis, and Y schal schewe to thee my feith of werkis.
059	JAM	002	019	Thou bileuest, that o God is; thou doist wel; and deuelis bileuen, and tremblen.
059	JAM	002	020	But wolt thou wite, thou veyn man, that feith with out werkis is idul?
059	JAM	002	021	Whether Abraham, oure fadir, was not iustified of werkis, offringe Ysaac, his sone, on the auter?
059	JAM	002	022	Therfor thou seest, that feith wrouyte with hise werkis, and his feith was fillid of werkis.
059	JAM	002	023	And the scripture was fillid, seiynge, Abraham bileuede to God, and it was arettid to hym to riytwisnesse, and he was clepid the freend of God.
059	JAM	002	024	Ye seen that a man is iustified of werkis, and not of feith oneli.
059	JAM	002	025	In lijk maner, and whether also Raab, the hoore, was not iustified of werkis, and resseyuede the messangeris, and sente hem out bi anothir weie?
059	JAM	002	026	For as the bodi with out spirit is deed, so also feith with out werkis is deed.
059	JAM	003	001	Mi britheren, nyle ye be maad many maistris, witynge that ye taken the more doom.
059	JAM	003	002	For alle we offenden in many thingis. If ony man offendith not in word, this is a perfit man; for also he may lede aboute al the bodi with a bridil.
059	JAM	003	003	For if we putten bridlis `in to horsis mouthis, for to consente to vs, and we leden aboute al the bodi of hem.
059	JAM	003	004	And lo! schippis, whanne thei ben grete, and ben dryuun of stronge wyndis, yit thei ben borun about of a litil gouernaile, where the meuyng of the gouernour wole.
059	JAM	003	005	So also the tunge is but a litil membre, and reisith grete thingis. Lo! hou litil fier brenneth a ful greet wode.
059	JAM	003	006	And oure tunge is fier, the vniuersite of wickidnesse. The tunge is ordeyned in oure membris, which defoulith al the bodi; and it is enflawmed of helle, and enflawmeth the wheel of oure birthe.
059	JAM	003	007	And al the kynde of beestis, and of foulis, and of serpentis, and of othere is chastisid, and tho ben maad tame of mannus kinde; but no man mai chastise the tunge,
059	JAM	003	008	for it is an vnpesible yuel, and ful of deedli venym.
059	JAM	003	009	In it we blessen God, the fadir, and in it we cursen men, that ben maad to the licnesse of God.
059	JAM	003	010	Of the same mouth passith forth blessing and cursing. My britheren, it bihoueth not that these thingis be don so.
059	JAM	003	011	Whether a welle of the same hoole bringith forth swete and salt watir?
059	JAM	003	012	My britheren, whether a fige tre may make grapis, ethir a vyne figus? So nethir salt watir mai make swete watir.
059	JAM	003	013	Who is wijs, and tauyt among you? schewe he of good lyuyng his worching, in myldenesse of his wisdom.
059	JAM	003	014	That if ye han bitter enuye, and stryuyngis ben in youre hertis, nyle ye haue glorye, and be lyeris ayens the treuthe.
059	JAM	003	015	For this wisdom is not fro aboue comynge doun, but ertheli, and beestli, and feendli.
059	JAM	003	016	For where is enuye and strijf, there is vnstidfastnesse and al schrewid werk.
059	JAM	003	017	But wisdom that is from aboue, first it is chast, aftirward pesible, mylde, able to be counseilid, consentinge to goode thingis, ful of merci and of goode fruytis, demynge with out feynyng.
059	JAM	003	018	And the fruyt of riytwisnesse is sowun in pees, to men that maken pees.
059	JAM	004	001	Wherof ben batelis and cheestis among you? Whether not of youre coueitisis, that fiyten in youre membris?
059	JAM	004	002	Ye coueiten, and ye han not; ye sleen, and ye han enuye, and ye moun not gete. Ye chiden, and maken batel; and ye han not, for ye axen not.
059	JAM	004	003	Ye axen, and ye resseyuen not; for that ye axen yuele, as ye schewen opynli in youre coueitisis.
059	JAM	004	004	Auowtreris, witen not ye, that the frenschip of this world is enemye to God? Therfor who euere wole be frend of this world, is maad the enemye of God.
059	JAM	004	005	Whether ye gessen, that the scripture seith veynli, The spirit that dwellith in you, coueitith to enuye?
059	JAM	004	006	But he yyueth the more grace; for which thing he seith, God withstondith proude men, but to meke men he yyueth grace.
059	JAM	004	007	Therfor be ye suget to God; but withstonde ye the deuel, and he schal fle fro you.
059	JAM	004	008	Neiye ye to God, and he schal neiye to you. Ye synneris, clense ye hondis, and ye double in soule, purge ye the hertis.
059	JAM	004	009	Be ye wretchis, and weile ye; youre leiyyng be turned in to weping, and ioye in to sorewe of herte.
059	JAM	004	010	Be ye mekid in the siyt of the Lord, and he schal enhaunse you.
059	JAM	004	011	My britheren, nyle ye bacbite ech othere. He that bacbitith his brothir, ethir that demeth his brothir, bacbitith the lawe, and demeth the lawe. And if thou demest the lawe, thou art not a doere of the lawe, but a domesman.
059	JAM	004	012	But oon is makere of the lawe, and iuge, that may lese, and delyuere.
059	JAM	004	013	And who art thou, that demest thi neiybore? Lo! now ye, that seien, To dai ethir to morewe we schulen go in to thilke citee, and there we schulen dwelle a yeer, and we schulen make marchaundise, and we schulen make wynning;
059	JAM	004	014	whiche witen not, what is to you in the morewe.
059	JAM	004	015	For what is youre lijf? A smoke apperinge at a litil, and aftirward it schal be wastid. Therfor that ye seie, If the Lord wole, and if we liuen, we schulen do this thing, ether that thing.
059	JAM	004	016	And now ye maken ful out ioye in youre pridis; euery siche ioye is wickyd.
059	JAM	004	017	Therfor it is synne to hym, that kan do good, and doith not.
059	JAM	005	001	Do now, ye riche men, wepe ye, yellinge in youre wretchidnessis that schulen come to you.
059	JAM	005	002	Youre richessis ben rotun, and youre clothis ben etun of mouytis.
059	JAM	005	003	Youre gold and siluer hath rustid, and the rust of hem schal be to you in to witnessyng, and schal ete youre fleischis, as fier. Ye han tresourid to you wraththe in the last daies.
059	JAM	005	004	Lo! the hire of youre werke men, that repiden youre feeldis, which is fraudid of you, crieth; and the cry of hem hath entrid in to the eeris of the Lord of oostis.
059	JAM	005	005	Ye han ete on the erthe, and in youre letcheries ye han nurschid youre hertis. In the dai of sleyng ye brouyten,
059	JAM	005	006	and slowen the iust man, and he ayenstood not you.
059	JAM	005	007	Therfor, britheren, be ye pacient, til to the comyng of the Lord. Lo! an erthetilier abidith preciouse fruyt of the erthe, paciently suffrynge, til he resseyue `tymeful and lateful fruyt.
059	JAM	005	008	And be ye pacient, and conferme ye youre hertis, for the comyng of the Lord schal neiye.
059	JAM	005	009	Britheren, nyle ye be sorewful ech to other, that ye be not demed. Lo! the iuge stondith niy bifor the yate.
059	JAM	005	010	Britheren, take ye ensaumple of yuel goyng out, and of long abidyng, and trauel, and of pacience, the prophetis, that speken to you in the name of the Lord.
059	JAM	005	011	Lo! we blessen hem that suffriden. Ye herden the `suffring, ethir pacience, of Joob, and ye sayn the ende of the Lord, for the Lord is merciful, and doynge merci.
059	JAM	005	012	Bifor alle thingis, my britheren, nyle ye swere, nether bi heuene, nether bi erthe, nethir bi what euere other ooth. But be youre word Yhe, yhe, Nay, nay, that ye fallen not vndir doom.
059	JAM	005	013	And if ony of you is sorewful, preye he with pacient soule, and seie he a salm.
059	JAM	005	014	If ony of you is sijk, lede he in preestis of the chirche, and preie thei for hym, and anoynte with oile in the name of the Lord;
059	JAM	005	015	and the preier of feith schal saue the sijk man, and the Lord schal make hym liyt; and if he be in synnes, thei schulen be foryouun to hym.
059	JAM	005	016	Therfor knouleche ye ech to othere youre synnes, and preye ye ech for othere, that ye be sauyd. For the contynuel preyer of a iust man is myche worth.
059	JAM	005	017	Elye was a deedli man lijk vs, and in preier he preiede, that it schulde not reyne on the erthe, and it reynede not thre yeeris and sixe monethis.
059	JAM	005	018	And eftsoone he preiede, and heuene yaf reyn, and the erthe yaf his fruyt.
059	JAM	005	019	And, britheren, if ony of you errith fro trewthe, and ony conuertith hym,
059	JAM	005	020	he owith to wite, that he that makith a synner to be turned fro the errour of his weye, schal saue the soule of hym fro deth, and keuereth the multitude of synnes.
